{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 47, "model_name": "meta/llama-4-maverick-17b-128e-instruct", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli was a nobleman and leader of the County of Tripoli a Crusader state in the Holy Land. He was born around 1140 and died in 1187. Raymond was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Jerusalem. After his father's death in 1152 Raymond II was succeeded by his son Raymond III who was still a minor at the time. \n\n*Early Life and Regency* Raymond's early life was marked by the regency of his mother Hodierna and later his cousin Amalric Count of Jaffa and Ascalon. In 1164 Raymond was taken captive by Nur ad-Din Zangi during the siege of Harim. He was released in 1174 after spending about 10 years in captivity. During his time in captivity Amalric acted as regent for the County of Tripoli.\n\nUpon his release Raymond regained control of his county and played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He was a strong supporter of his cousin Countess Sibylla of Jaffa and Ascalon and her husband William of Montferrat. In 1176 Raymond married Eschiva of Bures the widow of Walter of Saint Omer Prince of Galilee.\n\n*Marriage and Family* Raymond's marriage to Eschiva was childless and he had no heirs. This made his succession uncertain and he eventually adopted his godson Raymond of Antioch as his heir.\n\n*Later Life and Death* In 1187 Raymond died and was succeeded by his godson Raymond of Antioch who was still a minor at the time. Raymond's death was a significant blow to the Crusader states as he was one of the most experienced and respected leaders in the region. He was buried in the Church of the Holy Sepulchre in Tripoli. Raymond's legacy was marked by his efforts to defend the County of Tripoli against the growing threat of Muslim forces in the region. He played an important role in the politics of the Kingdom of Jerusalem and was a key figure in the events leading up to the Battl